import TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.Dispute

/-!
# TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.WhatForgivenessLeft — what forgiveness left unpaid, 203–1520

In 1547 the Council of Trent, in its decree on justification, condemned anyone
who says that after a sinner is justified "there remains not any debt of
temporal punishment to be discharged either in this world, or in the next in
Purgatory, before the entrance to the kingdom of heaven can be opened" (Session
VI, canon 30; Waterworth, p. 48). The same decree calls penance "a second plank
after the shipwreck of grace lost", crediting the phrase to "the holy Fathers",
and makes the penitent's fasts, alms and prayers a satisfaction "not indeed for
the eternal punishment, which is, together with the guilt, remitted", "but for
the temporal punishment" (ch. 14, pp. 41–42). That canon is why this chapter
belongs to sola fide. If faith alone receives a forgiveness that leaves nothing
owing, the canon is false; if something is still owed after forgiveness, faith
alone has not settled the account. This chapter asks where the teaching came
from, and tells it in the order it was written, up to the year Luther was
condemned.

Both sides in this story argue from Scripture. Gregory reads Matthew 12:32,
Aquinas the story of David, Clement VI the blood of Christ, and Luther the word
of Matthew 4:17 and its Greek. Rome also binds by councils and popes: Florence
defines, and Leo X grants indulgences "by the power of the keys". So on Rome's
side a teaching rests on Scripture and the church's teaching office together.
The rules each side later professed in its own words are
those of Trent's fourth session (1546) and the Westminster Confession (1647),
and the sola fide argument holds every party to the rule it professes.

## Satisfaction, from Tertullian to Lombard

The word is older than the doctrine. About 203 Tertullian, writing *On
Repentance*, allowed a baptised sinner one "second repentance", "once for all",
and told him "You have One whom you may satisfy, and Him willing" (*habes cui
satisfacias*; ch. 7, ANF 3, p. 663). In confession, he wrote, "satisfaction is
settled", and "by temporal mortification" the sinner may "expunge eternal
punishments" (ch. 9, p. 664). For Tertullian the penitent's suffering stood
against the eternal penalty, not a temporal one left over. Jerome, in letters
of the 390s to the 410s, called penitence "a plank for those who have had the
misfortune to be shipwrecked" (Letter 130.9, NPNF2 6, p. 266); the English of
those letters has no "second", which is Trent's word. In the 1150s Peter
Lombard, in the textbook every medieval theologian commented on, divided
penance into "compunction of heart, confession of mouth, satisfaction of deed"
(*compunctio cordis, confessio oris, satisfactio operis*; *Sentences* IV d. 16
c. 1, Quaracchi 1916, p. 839), and wrote that God alone frees the soul "from
the debt of eternal death" (d. 18 c. 6, p. 862).

Aquinas gave the distinction its reason. Writing the third part of his *Summa*
at Naples in 1272–1273, he took his text from the prophet Nathan:

> "Yes, and the Lord has forgiven your sin. You are not going to die.
> Nonetheless, because you have treated the Lord with such contempt in this
> matter, the son who has been born to you will certainly die." (2 Samuel
> 12:13–14, NET)

Sin, Aquinas explains, turns a man "from the immutable Good" and "inordinately
to mutable good". The first turning deserves eternal punishment and the second,
being finite, a punishment that is not eternal. So "when guilt is pardoned
through grace", "the debt of punishment is taken away, albeit a debt of some
temporal punishment may yet remain" (*Summa theologiae* III q. 86 a. 4).

## The fire after death

Christians prayed for their dead long before anyone defined what the prayers
did. About 211 Tertullian wrote, "As often as the anniversary comes round, we
make offerings for the dead as birthday honours" (*De corona* 3, ANF 3, p. 94).
Augustine read the fire of 1 Corinthians 3:15, "saved, but only as through
fire" (NET), of this life: "the grief for what he loved and has lost burns
him". Whether there is such a fire after death he left open: "It is a matter
that may be inquired into, and either ascertained or left doubtful, whether
some believers shall pass through a kind of purgatorial fire" (*Enchiridion*
68–69, about 421; NPNF1 3, pp. 259–260).

Gregory the Great closed the question in 593 or 594. In the fourth book of his
*Dialogues*, among accounts of souls seen and heard after death, he wrote that
"we must believe that before the day of judgment there is a Purgatory fire for
certain small sins" (*purgatorius ignis credendus est*), because the Lord says
the sin against the Spirit "will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the
age to come" (Matthew 12:32, NET). He granted that 1 Corinthians 3 "may be
understood of the fire of tribulation, which men suffer in this world" (IV.39,
Gardner's edition, pp. 232–234). In 1439 the Council of Florence defined it:
"if truly penitent people die in the love of God before they have made
satisfaction for acts and omissions by worthy fruits of repentance, their souls
are cleansed after death by cleansing pains" (*Laetentur caeli*). Purgatory, so
defined, is satisfaction not yet made.

## The treasury

In 1095 Urban II promised at Clermont that whoever went to Jerusalem "through
devotion alone" would find that "the journey itself shall take the place of all
penance" (canon 2; Munro 1901, p. 12). The church could release the penalty a
penitent owed. In the next two centuries the schoolmen asked what it released
it from, and answered with a treasury. Aquinas, in his commentary on the
*Sentences* (1252–1256), wrote that indulgences avail through "the oneness of the
mystical body": the merits of Christ and the saints are "the common property of
the whole Church", and the pope has "authority to dispense this treasure"
(IV d. 20, printed as *Summa*, Supplement q. 25 aa. 1–2). In 1343 Clement VI made
it the teaching of a papal bull. Christ, *Unigenitus* says, "poured out not a
little drop of blood, which however on account of union with the Word would
have been sufficient for the redemption of the whole human race, but copiously";
this treasure He "committed to be dispensed" through Peter and his successors
"for ... remission of temporal punishment due to sins", and the merits of Mary
"and of all the elect" add to it (Denzinger 550–552). In 1476 Sixtus IV granted
the souls in purgatory "a plenary remission ... as a suffrage", by way of
prayer for them (*Salvator noster*, Denzinger 723a).

## Luther, 1517–1518

Luther's theses of October 1517 begin with the word of Matthew 4:17, "Repent,
for the kingdom of heaven is near!" (NET). Christ "willed that the whole life of
believers should be repentance" (thesis 1), and "this word cannot be understood
to mean sacramental penance, i. e., confession and satisfaction, which is
administered by the priests" (thesis 2; *Works of Martin Luther* vol. 1, p. 29).
In May 1518 he told Staupitz why. The Greek *metanoia*, "repentance", is "a
'coming to one's senses'", while the Latin *poenitentiam agere*, "to do
penance", indicates "an action, rather than a change of heart, and [is] in no
way an equivalent for the Greek" (pp. 40–41).

The theses grant more than a reader of the later Luther expects. They take
purgatory for granted, and argue only over what the pope can do for the souls
in it (theses 15–29), though they call the turning of the church's penalties
into the penalty of purgatory "one of the tares that were sown while the
bishops slept" (thesis 11, p. 30). They do not deny a penalty after
forgiveness: "the penalty [of sin], therefore, continues so long as hatred of
self continues" (thesis 4). What they deny is the treasury. The treasures of the
church are "not the merits of Christ and the Saints, for even without the pope,
these always work grace for the inner man, and the cross, death, and hell for the
outward man" (thesis 58), and "the true treasure of the Church is the Most Holy
Gospel of the glory and the grace of God" (thesis 62, p. 35).

Leo X answered in November 1518. *Cum postquam* defines that the pope, "by the
power of the keys", removes "the fault by means of the sacrament of penance, but
the temporal punishment due for actual sins according to divine justice by
means of the indulgence of the Church", which he grants "from the superabundant
merits of Christ and the saints", to the living and, "by means of suffrage", to
the dead (Denzinger 740a). In June 1520 *Exsurge Domine* condemned, among
Luther's propositions, "Indulgences are pious frauds of the faithful" and
"Purgatory cannot be proved from Sacred Scripture which is in the canon".

## Who stands, to 1520

Every position in the first hearing cites only Scripture and works written
before 1521 (`forgiveness_heard_before_1521`). Gregory, Aquinas and Florence
stand: nothing in the hearing denies what they hold, because Luther in 1517
grants purgatory and a penalty after forgiveness. Luther and the two popes
defeat each other over the treasury, on steps each rated `disputed`, so none of
the three stands (`to_1520_purgatory_stands_and_the_treasury_ties`). Where Luther
and Clement part is thesis 58 alone: Luther grants the debt Clement builds on,
and denies only that the merits of Christ and the saints are a treasure the pope
dispenses (`whereLutherMeetsClement`). Luther's
reading of Matthew 4:17 meets no one, because no voice of these years is read
here holding the repentance of that verse to be the sacrament, and the reading
Luther rejects is his own report of it. One reading holds Gregory, Aquinas,
Florence and Luther together (`in_1517_luther_held_purgatory`).

## What the history means

The facts are not in dispute: the prayers for the dead are early, the fire
after death is an open question in Augustine and an article of belief in
Gregory, and the treasury is a doctrine of the thirteenth century bound by
popes in the fourteenth. The two sides read the same facts two ways.

Martin Chemnitz, the Lutheran theologian of Brunswick, read them as an addition.
Gregory, he wrote, "introduced into the Church another and new way of proving
and confirming dogmas of the faith, namely seeking the truth from the dead"; let
the reader note that what Rome teaches of souls in purgatory is "not ancient,
but first revealed in Gregory's time" (*non esse antiqua, sed tempore Gregorii
primum revelata*; *Examen* III, Preuss's edition, p. 628, in our translation).
John Henry Newman read them as development. Purgatory, he granted, "can show
little definite, or at least but partial, testimony" before Augustine, but
"some notion of suffering, or disadvantage, or punishment after this life, in
the case of the faithful departed", has "almost a *consensus* of the four first
ages of the Church" (*Essay on Development*, Introduction §15, revised text of
1878, p. 22). "Thus we see how, as time went on, the doctrine of Purgatory was
brought home to the minds of the faithful as a portion or form of Penance due
for post-baptismal sin" (ch. IX §4, p. 390).

Each grants the other's facts and denies the other's conclusion, so each step
is rated `disputed`, and the two defeat each other
(`what_the_history_means_is_a_standoff`), so on these premises neither reading of
the history stands. Whether any debt remains after justification is the question
Trent's canon 30 put to the Reformers in 1547, after the story this chapter tells.
-/

namespace TestimonyArguments.SolaFide

open Testimony

open Testimony Testimony.Bib Testimony.Logic Testimony.Logic.Framework Testimony.Scripture

/-! ### Gregory, 593–594 -/

/-- **Who draws the step from Matthew 12:32 to a cleansing after death.** Gregory
himself: "we must believe that before the day of judgment there is a Purgatory
fire for certain small sins: because our Saviour saith, that he which speaketh
blasphemy against the holy Ghost, that it shall not be forgiven him, neither in
this world, nor in the world to come" (*Dialogues* IV.39, p. 233). Rated
`disputed`, because Calvin grants the saying and denies that it bears on
purgatory: "who sees not that the Lord there speaks of the guilt of sin? But if
this is so, what has it to do with their purgatory, seeing they deny not that
the guilt of those sins, the punishment of which is there expiated, is forgiven
in the present life?" (*Institutes* III.5.7). -/
def gregoryPurgatoryStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work gregoryDialogues (.sectionRef "IV.39; Gardner, p. 233")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **A fire before the judgment, from Matthew 12:32 (Gregory).** The sin against
the Spirit "will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come"
(Matthew 12:32, NET). So some sins are forgiven in the age to come, and there is
a cleansing fire after death for small sins. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def gregoryPurgatoryLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "A fire before the judgment, from Matthew 12:32 (Gregory)"
  , grounds := [p .matthew12_32NotForgivenInTheAgeToCome]
  , step := p .matthew12_32NotForgivenInTheAgeToCome ➝ p .soulsAreCleansedAfterDeath
  , delivers := p .soulsAreCleansedAfterDeath
  , inference := some gregoryPurgatoryStep }

/-- Gregory's position: a cleansing after death, from Matthew 12:32. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def gregoryCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  gregoryPurgatoryLine.asPackage baseCite "souls are cleansed after death"

/-! ### Aquinas, 1272–1273 -/

/-- **Who draws the step to a debt that remains.** Aquinas: "when guilt is
pardoned through grace, the soul ceases to be turned away from God ... so that
at the same time, the debt of punishment is taken away, albeit a debt of some
temporal punishment may yet remain" (*Summa theologiae* III q. 86 a. 4), with
David as his text. Rated `disputed`, because Calvin grants that David was
forgiven and his son died, and denies that a debt remained: "if we are freed
from guilt by Christ, the punishment consequent upon guilt must cease with it"
(*Institutes* III.4.30). David's loss was "not properly punishment or vengeance,
but correction and admonition", the act "of a father", not "of a judge"
(III.4.31). -/
def aquinasDebtStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work aquinasTertiaPars (.sectionRef "q. 86 a. 4")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **A debt after forgiveness (Aquinas).** The Lord forgave David's sin, and
still "the son who has been born to you will certainly die" (2 Samuel 12:13–14,
NET). Sin turns a man from God and to a changeable good, and grace, uniting him
to God, ends the first; the second, being finite, leaves a finite debt. So when
guilt is forgiven a debt of temporal punishment may remain. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def aquinasDebtLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "A debt of temporal punishment after forgiveness (Aquinas)"
  , grounds := [p .secondSamuel12_13_14ForgivenYetPunished, p .sinTurnsToAMutableGood]
  , step := ⋀ [p .secondSamuel12_13_14ForgivenYetPunished, p .sinTurnsToAMutableGood] ➝
      p .temporalDebtRemainsAfterForgiveness
  , delivers := p .temporalDebtRemainsAfterForgiveness
  , inference := some aquinasDebtStep }

/-- Aquinas's position: a debt of temporal punishment may remain after the guilt
is forgiven. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def aquinasDebtCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  aquinasDebtLine.asPackage baseCite "a debt of temporal punishment remains after forgiveness"

/-! ### Clement VI, 1343 -/

/-- **Who draws the step from Christ's merit to the treasury.** Clement VI: from
the blood Christ shed "copiously", "how great a treasure did the good Father
acquire from this for the Church militant", and "this treasure ... He has
committed to be dispensed" through Peter and his successors "for ... remission
of temporal punishment due to sins" (*Unigenitus*, Denzinger 550–551). Rated
`disputed`, because Luther grants the merits of Christ and denies that they are
the treasure of indulgences: they "always work grace for the inner man" "even
without the pope" (thesis 58). -/
def clementTreasuryStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work clementUnigenitus (.sectionRef "Denzinger 550–552")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- The grounds the treasury is drawn from: Christ's merit, more than redemption
needed, and the debt of temporal punishment it is dispensed to remit. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def treasuryGrounds : List (Formula Claim) :=
  [p .christsMeritSuperabounds, p .temporalDebtRemainsAfterForgiveness]

/-- **The treasury of merits (Clement VI).** Christ shed far more of His blood
than the one drop that would have redeemed the human race, and the forgiven
still owe a temporal punishment. So the merits of Christ, with those of Mary and
the saints, are a treasure the pope dispenses to remit that punishment. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def clementTreasuryLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The treasury of merits (Clement VI, Unigenitus)"
  , grounds := treasuryGrounds
  , step := ⋀ treasuryGrounds ➝ p .treasuryIsMeritsOfChristAndSaints
  , delivers := p .treasuryIsMeritsOfChristAndSaints
  , inference := some clementTreasuryStep }

/-- Clement VI's position: the treasure of indulgences is the merits of Christ
and the saints. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def clementTreasuryCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  clementTreasuryLine.asPackage baseCite "the treasure of indulgences is the merits of Christ"

/-! ### Florence, 1439 -/

/-- **Who draws the step from the debt to purgatory.** The council: the souls of
the truly penitent who die "before they have made satisfaction for acts and
omissions by worthy fruits of repentance" "are cleansed after death by
cleansing pains" (*Laetentur caeli*). Rated `disputed`, because Melanchthon in
1531 grants that God still punishes a sin He has forgiven and denies that
purgatory follows: "Upon David a peculiar punishment was imposed, therefore, in
addition to common afflictions, there is another punishment of purgatory", he
writes, is a rule that does not follow (*Apology* XII, 59). Calvin grants the
link and denies the debt: "what is this purgatory but the satisfaction for sin
paid after death by the souls of the dead? Hence when this idea of satisfaction
is refuted, purgatory itself is forthwith completely overturned" (*Institutes*
III.5.6). -/
def florencePurgatoryStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work florenceLaetentur (.sectionRef "Session 6, on purgatory")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Purgatory as satisfaction not yet made (Florence).** A debt of temporal
punishment may remain after the guilt is forgiven. So the penitent who die
before they have paid it are cleansed after death. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def florencePurgatoryLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Purgatory as satisfaction not yet made (Florence)"
  , grounds := [p .temporalDebtRemainsAfterForgiveness]
  , step := p .temporalDebtRemainsAfterForgiveness ➝ p .soulsAreCleansedAfterDeath
  , delivers := p .soulsAreCleansedAfterDeath
  , inference := some florencePurgatoryStep }

/-- Florence's position: the souls of penitents who die before making
satisfaction are cleansed after death. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def florencePurgatoryCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  florencePurgatoryLine.asPackage baseCite "souls are cleansed after death"

/-! ### Luther, 1517–1518 -/

/-- **Who draws the step against the treasury.** Luther, in thesis 58: the
treasures of the church are "not the merits of Christ and the Saints, for even
without the pope, these always work grace for the inner man, and the cross,
death, and hell for the outward man" (*Works* vol. 1, p. 35). Rated `disputed`,
because Leo X grants the merits of Christ and the saints and defines that the
pope grants indulgences "from the superabundant merits of Christ and the
saints" (*Cum postquam*, Denzinger 740a). -/
def lutherTreasuryStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work lutherNinetyFiveTheses (.sectionRef "thesis 58; Works 1, p. 35")
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Not the merits of Christ (Luther, thesis 58).** The merits of Christ and
the saints work grace for the inner man without the pope. So they are not the
treasure from which the pope grants indulgences. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lutherTreasuryLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Not the merits of Christ (Luther, thesis 58)"
  , grounds := [p .christsMeritsWorkGraceWithoutThePope]
  , step := p .christsMeritsWorkGraceWithoutThePope ➝ notP .treasuryIsMeritsOfChristAndSaints
  , delivers := notP .treasuryIsMeritsOfChristAndSaints
  , inference := some lutherTreasuryStep }

/-- **Who draws the step from the Greek to the meaning of Matthew 4:17.** Luther,
in thesis 2, and in his letter to Staupitz, where he calls false the teachers
who "had derived their idea from the Latin words *poenitentiam agere*, which
indicate an action, rather than a change of heart, and are in no way an
equivalent for the Greek *metanoia*" (*Works* vol. 1, p. 41). Rated
`wellSupported`: no reader has been read who grants what Luther says the Greek
word means and still reads the verse of the sacrament. The Rheims annotators of
1582, who keep "do penance", deny the meaning itself: if Protestants "pretend
the Greeke word", the places where it is used show that it "must needes
signifie, sorowful, payneful, and satisfactorie repentance" (on Matthew 3:2). -/
def lutherRepentStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work lutherNinetyFiveTheses (.sectionRef "theses 1–2; Works 1, p. 29")
  , supporting := [.work lutherToStaupitz (.sectionRef "Works 1, pp. 40–41")]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- **Repent, not do penance (Luther, theses 1–2).** Jesus preached "Repent"
(μετανοεῖτε, Matthew 4:17, NET), and the Greek word means a change of heart, "a
'coming to one's senses'", not an act done. So the repentance He commands "cannot
be understood to mean sacramental penance, i. e., confession and satisfaction"
(thesis 2). -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lutherRepentLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Repent, not do penance (Luther, theses 1–2)"
  , grounds := [p .matthew4_17Repent, p .metanoiaIsAChangeOfHeart]
  , step := ⋀ [p .matthew4_17Repent, p .metanoiaIsAChangeOfHeart] ➝
      notP .matthew4_17MeansSacramentalPenance
  , delivers := notP .matthew4_17MeansSacramentalPenance
  , inference := some lutherRepentStep }

/-- Luther's position in 1517–1518: the treasury of indulgences is not the merits
of Christ, and the repentance of Matthew 4:17 is not the sacrament of penance. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lutherThesesCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Luther's theses (1517)"
  , cite := baseCite
  , premises := caseOf [lutherTreasuryLine, lutherRepentLine] [] []
  , conclusion := notP .treasuryIsMeritsOfChristAndSaints
  , conclusionLabel := "the treasure of indulgences is not the merits of Christ"
  , inferences := [lutherTreasuryStep, lutherRepentStep] }

/-! ### Leo X, 1518 -/

/-- **Who draws the step to the treasury in 1518.** Leo X, in *Cum postquam*: the
pope removes "the temporal punishment due for actual sins according to divine
justice by means of the indulgence of the Church", which he grants "from the
superabundant merits of Christ and the saints" (Denzinger 740a). Rated
`disputed`, for the reason Clement's step is: Luther's thesis 58 grants the
merits and denies that they are the treasure. -/
def leoTreasuryStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work leoCumPostquam (.sectionRef "Denzinger 740a")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **The treasury defined against Luther (Leo X, Cum postquam).** The same
grounds as Clement's, Christ's superabundant merit and the temporal punishment
still owed, and the same conclusion, defined to be "held by all" under pain of
excommunication. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def leoTreasuryLine : Line Claim :=
  { clementTreasuryLine with
    name := "The treasury defined against Luther (Leo X, Cum postquam)"
    inference := some leoTreasuryStep }

/-- Leo X's position: the treasure of indulgences is the superabundant merits of
Christ and the saints. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def leoTreasuryCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  leoTreasuryLine.asPackage baseCite "the treasure of indulgences is the merits of Christ"

/-! ### The story to 1520, weighed -/

/-- The parties to the story to 1520. -/
inductive ForgivenessParty
  /-- Gregory the Great, 593–594: a cleansing fire after death, from Matthew
  12:32. -/
  | gregory
  /-- Aquinas, 1272–1273: a debt of temporal punishment after forgiveness. -/
  | aquinas
  /-- Clement VI, 1343: the treasury of the merits of Christ and the saints. -/
  | clement
  /-- The Council of Florence, 1439: purgatory as satisfaction not yet made. -/
  | florence
  /-- Luther, 1517–1518: not the merits of Christ, and repent, not do penance. -/
  | luther
  /-- Leo X, 1518: the treasury defined. -/
  | leo
deriving DecidableEq

/-- The position each party argues from. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def forgivenessNode : ForgivenessParty → ArgumentPackage Claim
  | .gregory => gregoryCase
  | .aquinas => aquinasDebtCase
  | .clement => clementTreasuryCase
  | .florence => florencePurgatoryCase
  | .luther => lutherThesesCase
  | .leo => leoTreasuryCase

/-- The story of what forgiveness left unpaid, to 1520. -/
declare_dispute forgivenessDispute := forgivenessNode using solaFideDefs
  /-- **Who defeats whom.** Luther and each of the two popes defeat each other
  over the treasury; nothing else attacks anything. Each pair is checked by the
  proof checker. -/
  defeats [ (.clement, .luther), (.luther, .clement), (.luther, .leo), (.leo, .luther) ]
  /-- Aquinas lends the debt after forgiveness that he concludes to Clement, to
  Florence and to Leo, each of whom builds on it. Each pair is checked by the
  proof checker. -/
  supports [(.aquinas, .clement), (.aquinas, .florence), (.aquinas, .leo)]
  /-- Leo's position is Clement's, defined again: each is contained in the other.
  Each pair is checked by the proof checker. -/
  partOf [(.clement, .leo), (.leo, .clement)]
  /-- The story to 1520 drawn: who defeats whom. -/
  map forgivenessMap

/-- **The story to 1520 hears no later voice.** Every reference each party cites,
for its claims and for its steps, is Scripture or a work dated before 1521. -/
theorem forgiveness_heard_before_1521 :
    forgivenessDispute.citesOnlyBefore .gregory 1521 = true ∧
      forgivenessDispute.citesOnlyBefore .aquinas 1521 = true ∧
      forgivenessDispute.citesOnlyBefore .clement 1521 = true ∧
      forgivenessDispute.citesOnlyBefore .florence 1521 = true ∧
      forgivenessDispute.citesOnlyBefore .luther 1521 = true ∧
      forgivenessDispute.citesOnlyBefore .leo 1521 = true := by
  decide +kernel

/-- **What forgiveness left unpaid, told in the order it was written.** Where a
turn meets an earlier one, the two defeat each other, as the solver computes
from their premises. -/
declare_bundle forgivenessExchange in forgivenessDispute.finite where
  /-- In 593 or 594 Gregory writes that "we must believe that before the day of
  judgment there is a Purgatory fire for certain small sins", from Matthew
  12:32. -/
  turn ForgivenessParty.gregory from gregoryDialogues
  /-- In 1272–1273 Aquinas, from David's forgiveness and his son's death, holds
  that "a debt of some temporal punishment may yet remain". -/
  turn ForgivenessParty.aquinas from aquinasTertiaPars
  /-- In 1343 Clement VI teaches that the blood Christ shed "copiously" is a
  treasure the pope dispenses "for ... remission of temporal punishment". -/
  turn ForgivenessParty.clement from clementUnigenitus
  /-- In 1439 the Council of Florence defines that the penitent who die before
  making satisfaction "are cleansed after death by cleansing pains". -/
  turn ForgivenessParty.florence from florenceLaetentur
  /-- In 1517 Luther denies that the treasures of the church are "the merits of
  Christ and the Saints" (thesis 58), and reads "Repent" as a change of heart,
  not the sacrament (theses 1–2). The treasury is where he and Clement meet. -/
  turn ForgivenessParty.luther meets ForgivenessParty.clement from lutherNinetyFiveTheses
  /-- In November 1518 Leo X defines that the pope grants indulgences "from the
  superabundant merits of Christ and the saints". -/
  turn ForgivenessParty.leo meets ForgivenessParty.luther from leoCumPostquam

/-- The reading of 1517: Luther's, held with the voices before him. Souls are
cleansed after death, a debt of temporal punishment remains after forgiveness,
the treasury of indulgences is not the merits of Christ, and the repentance of
Matthew 4:17 is not the sacrament. Every other claim is held true on it, except
those the argument's meanings exclude: baptism as the instrument, James's word
as Paul's, and the Latin of Revelation 22:11 and Sirach 18:22. -/
def lutherIn1517Reading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .treasuryIsMeritsOfChristAndSaints => False
  | .matthew4_17MeansSacramentalPenance => False
  | .baptismIsInstrumentalCause => False
  | .jamesUsesDikaioAsPaul => False
  | .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill => False
  | .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath => False
  | .energoumeneIsPassive => False
  | .loveMovesFaith => False
  | .justificationHasDegrees => False
  | .worksCauseIncreaseOfJustification => False
  | .justificationIncludesSanctification => False
  | .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity => False
  | .paulsJustifyDenotesRenewal => False
  | _ => True

/-- **In 1517 Luther held purgatory with Gregory, Aquinas and Florence.** One
reading satisfies all four positions: souls are cleansed after death, a debt of
temporal punishment remains after forgiveness, and the treasury of indulgences
is not the merits of Christ. -/
theorem in_1517_luther_held_purgatory :
    forgivenessDispute.StandTogether [.gregory, .aquinas, .florence, .luther] := by
  satisfied_by lutherIn1517Reading [Dispute.StandTogether, solaFideDefs]

/-- Luther's theses deliver what they conclude. -/
theorem lutherThesesCase_establishes : Establishes lutherThesesCase := by
  establish [solaFideDefs]

/-- Luther's theses can be held without contradiction: the reading of 1517 holds
every premise. -/
theorem lutherThesesCase_is_satisfiable : Satisfiable lutherThesesCase.premises := by
  satisfied_by lutherIn1517Reading [solaFideDefs]

/-- **Where Luther and Clement VI part.** Everything turns on Luther's step in
thesis 58: the merits of Christ and the saints work grace "even without the
pope", so they are not the treasure the pope dispenses. What that contradicts is
the whole of Clement's case, and each part is needed: Christ's blood shed more
than redemption needed, the debt of temporal punishment left after forgiveness,
and Clement's step from the two to the treasury. Luther in 1517 grants the debt,
so the break is over the treasury and nothing before it. The step is rated
`disputed`, because Leo X grants the merits of Christ and the saints and defines
that the pope grants indulgences from them (*Cum postquam*). -/
def whereLutherMeetsClement : Because lutherThesesCase clementTreasuryCase :=
  Because.ofChecks lutherTreasuryLine.step
    [p .christsMeritsWorkGraceWithoutThePope, p .matthew4_17Repent, p .metanoiaIsAChangeOfHeart]
    [lutherRepentLine.step]
    [p .christsMeritsWorkGraceWithoutThePope]
    [ p .christsMeritSuperabounds, p .temporalDebtRemainsAfterForgiveness
    , clementTreasuryLine.step ]
    .derives lutherThesesCase_establishes lutherThesesCase_is_satisfiable
    (by simp [lutherThesesCase, caseOf, lutherTreasuryLine, lutherRepentLine])
    (by simp [lutherThesesCase, caseOf, lutherTreasuryLine, lutherRepentLine])
    (by simp [clementTreasuryCase, clementTreasuryLine, treasuryGrounds, Line.asPackage,
      Line.premises])
    (by decide +kernel)
  |>.ratedBy lutherTreasuryStep (by simp [lutherThesesCase, solaFideDefs])

/-- Why Gregory, Aquinas and Florence stand and the treasury's three voices do
not: nothing attacks the first three, and Luther and each pope defeat each
other. -/
declare_verdict forgivenessSettles for forgivenessDispute :
    .groundedExactly [[.gregory, .aquinas, .florence]]
      [(.clement, .luther), (.luther, .clement), (.leo, .luther)]
  /-- **To 1520, purgatory and the debt after forgiveness stand, and the treasury
  ties.** Gregory's fire after death, Aquinas's debt of temporal punishment and
  Florence's purgatory are attacked by nothing heard before 1521, because Luther
  in 1517 grants them. Luther's thesis 58 denies that the treasure of
  indulgences is the merits of Christ and the saints, which Clement VI taught
  and Leo X defined, and each step is rated `disputed`, so Luther and each pope
  defeat each other and none of the three stands. -/
  proves @[headline] to_1520_purgatory_stands_and_the_treasury_ties

#print axioms to_1520_purgatory_stands_and_the_treasury_ties

/-! ### What the history means -/

/-- **Who draws the step from the late testimony to an addition.** Chemnitz: "let
the reader note in this question that what the papists teach of the state of
souls in purgatory is not ancient, but first revealed in Gregory's time"
(*Examen* III, p. 628; our translation). Rated `disputed`, because Newman grants
that purgatory "can show little definite, or at least but partial, testimony"
before Augustine, and holds it developed "out of earlier points of faith"
(*Essay*, Introduction §15, p. 22; ch. IX §4, p. 390). -/
def chemnitzAccretionStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work chemnitzExamen (.page 628)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- The late testimony Chemnitz reads: Augustine left the fire after death an
open question, and Gregory first wrote that it must be believed, among accounts
of souls seen after death. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lateTestimony : List (Formula Claim) :=
  [p .augustineLeftTheFireOpen, p .gregoryTaughtItFromVisions]

/-- **Not ancient, but first revealed in Gregory's time (Chemnitz).** Augustine
could not say whether there is a cleansing fire after death; Gregory was the
first to say it must be believed, and he "introduced into the Church another and
new way of proving and confirming dogmas of the faith, namely seeking the truth
from the dead". So purgatory is not the ancient faith of the church. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def chemnitzAccretionLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Not ancient, but first revealed in Gregory's time (Chemnitz)"
  , grounds := lateTestimony
  , step := ⋀ lateTestimony ➝ notP .purgatoryIsAncientTeaching
  , delivers := notP .purgatoryIsAncientTeaching
  , inference := some chemnitzAccretionStep }

/-- Chemnitz's position: purgatory is an addition. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def chemnitzAccretionCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  chemnitzAccretionLine.asPackage baseCite "purgatory is not the ancient faith"

/-- **Who draws the step from the early prayers to a development.** Newman: some
notion of "punishment after this life, in the case of the faithful departed",
has "almost a *consensus* of the four first ages of the Church", and "as time
went on, the doctrine of Purgatory was brought home to the minds of the faithful
as a portion or form of Penance due for post-baptismal sin" (*Essay*,
Introduction §15, p. 22; ch. IX §4, p. 390). Rated `disputed`, because Calvin
grants that Augustine prayed for his mother and denies that it shows purgatory:
it was "doubtless an old woman's wish, which her son did not bring to the test
of Scripture" (*Institutes* III.5.10); and Chemnitz grants the early prayers and
denies the doctrine is ancient. -/
def newmanDevelopmentStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work newmanDevelopment1878 (.sectionRef "Introduction §15, p. 22")
  , supporting := [.work newmanDevelopment1878 (.sectionRef "ch. IX §4, p. 390")]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Held in vaguer forms, stated more fully (Newman).** The church made
offerings for the dead from the early third century. So purgatory belongs to its
faith from the beginning, held at first in vaguer forms and stated more fully as
time went on. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def newmanDevelopmentLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Held in vaguer forms, stated more fully (Newman)"
  , grounds := [p .earlyChurchPrayedForTheDead]
  , step := p .earlyChurchPrayedForTheDead ➝ p .purgatoryIsAncientTeaching
  , delivers := p .purgatoryIsAncientTeaching
  , inference := some newmanDevelopmentStep }

/-- Newman's position: purgatory is a development of the ancient faith. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def newmanDevelopmentCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  newmanDevelopmentLine.asPackage baseCite "purgatory belongs to the ancient faith"

/-- The two readings of the history. -/
inductive HistoryParty
  /-- Chemnitz, 1566: an addition, first revealed in Gregory's time. -/
  | chemnitz
  /-- Newman, 1878: a development of what the church held from the first. -/
  | newman
deriving DecidableEq

/-- The position each reading argues from. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def historyNode : HistoryParty → ArgumentPackage Claim
  | .chemnitz => chemnitzAccretionCase
  | .newman => newmanDevelopmentCase

/-- What the history of purgatory means. -/
declare_dispute historyDispute := historyNode using solaFideDefs
  /-- **Who defeats whom.** Chemnitz and Newman defeat each other. Each pair is
  checked by the proof checker. -/
  defeats [(.chemnitz, .newman), (.newman, .chemnitz)]
  /-- Neither lends the other a premise. Each pair is checked by the proof
  checker. -/
  supports []
  /-- Neither is contained in the other. Each pair is checked by the proof
  checker. -/
  partOf []
  /-- What the history means, drawn: who defeats whom. -/
  map historyMap

/-- **The two readings, in the order they were written.** -/
declare_bundle historyExchange in historyDispute.finite where
  /-- Chemnitz: what Rome teaches of purgatory is "not ancient, but first
  revealed in Gregory's time". -/
  turn HistoryParty.chemnitz from chemnitzExamen
  /-- Newman: "as time went on, the doctrine of Purgatory was brought home to
  the minds of the faithful as a portion or form of Penance". -/
  turn HistoryParty.newman meets HistoryParty.chemnitz from newmanDevelopment1878

/-- Why neither reading stands: each defeats the other. -/
declare_verdict historySettles for historyDispute :
    .nothingGrounded [(.chemnitz, .newman), (.newman, .chemnitz)]
  /-- **What the history of purgatory means is a standoff.** Chemnitz reads the
  late testimony, Augustine's open question and Gregory's "must be believed", as
  an addition; Newman reads the early prayers for the dead as the seed of a
  development. Each grants the other's facts and denies the other's conclusion,
  so each step is rated `disputed`, and the two defeat each other. -/
  proves @[headline] what_the_history_means_is_a_standoff

#print axioms what_the_history_means_is_a_standoff

end TestimonyArguments.SolaFide
